---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { getGuide } from "../../data/guides";

const guide = getGuide("isolation");
---

<DocsLayout guide={guide}>
<section class="docs-hero docs-searchable" id="overview" data-search="isolation docker sandbox untrusted repository network read only">
          <p class="docs-kicker">Isolation</p>
          <h1>Give the scan<br /><span>its own boundary.</span></h1>
          <p>
            Docker is repyy’s opt-in analysis backend. Review its limits before relying on the result.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-summary" aria-label="Isolation summary">
            <span><b>Docker</b>Opt-in release image</span><span><b>Input</b>Read-only mount requested
      </span>
          </div>
          <div class="docs-callout">
            <strong>Isolation changes where analysis runs</strong>
            <p>
              It does not make a repository safe. Keep credentials, package caches, home directories, IDE trust, dependency installs, builds, and tests outside
              the review boundary.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="docker" data-search="docker sandbox ghcr digest release image">
          <h2>Docker: use the signed release image</h2>
          <p>
            Docker mode is opt-in. Start Docker Desktop on macOS or Windows, or Docker Engine on Linux, and verify the daemon responds to
            <code>docker version</code>. Releases with Docker support include <code>repyy-sandbox-image.txt</code> with the exact image reference and digest.
            Run{" "}
      <code>repyy version</code>, open the matching release, and use its published digest. Do not substitute mutable <code>latest</code>.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-command">
            <div>
              <span>Pull and inspect</span
              ><button
                type="button"
                data-copy="docker pull ghcr.io/kevin-umali/repyy-sandbox@sha256:<published-digest>; docker image inspect ghcr.io/kevin-umali/repyy-sandbox@sha256:<published-digest>"
              >
                Copy
              </button>
            </div>
            <code
              >docker pull ghcr.io/kevin-umali/repyy-sandbox@sha256:&lt;published-digest&gt;<br />docker image inspect
              ghcr.io/kevin-umali/repyy-sandbox@sha256:&lt;published-digest&gt;</code
            >
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="docker-image" data-search="cosign sigstore verify signature certificate identity oidc checksum">
          <h2>Verify the image signature</h2>
          <p>
            Follow
            <a href="../verification/#container-verification">Releases and Verification</a> to authenticate the image and binary before use. Repyy checks that
            the pinned image is present locally; it does not verify signatures during a scan.
          </p>
          <p>
            A missing daemon or pinned image is a preflight error; repyy never silently falls back to host mode. Checkout deletion failures mark scans
            incomplete while preserving findings. Credential-file and forced-container cleanup failures also surface as errors.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section
          class="docs-section docs-searchable"
          id="local"
          data-search="docker local path read only mount networking disabled analysis container json report"
        >
          <h2>Docker with a local folder</h2>
          <p>
            repyy requests a read-only input mount and <code>--network none</code> for the analysis container, and writes bounded JSON back to the host. The host
            validates the result and writes the final report.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-command">
            <div>
              <span>Local scan</span
              ><button type="button" data-copy="repyy scan ./untrusted-repository --sandbox=docker --format html --output repyy-docker-report.html">
                Copy
              </button>
            </div>
            <code>repyy scan ./untrusted-repository --sandbox=docker --format html --output repyy-docker-report.html</code>
          </div>
          <p>Keep output in a narrow directory and treat it as sensitive local review data.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="docs-section docs-searchable" id="https" data-search="docker https remote fetch stage shallow clone network credentials token git">
          <h2>Docker with an HTTPS remote</h2>
          <p>
            An HTTPS remote uses a disposable networked fetch stage for a shallow clone. A separate analysis stage receives no Git credentials and requests{" "}
      <code>--network none</code>. The
            host validates the bounded result before rendering the report.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-command">
            <div>
              <span>HTTPS remote</span
              ><button type="button" data-copy="repyy scan https://github.com/org/repository --sandbox=docker --format html --output report.html">Copy</button>
            </div>
            <code>repyy scan https://github.com/org/repository --sandbox=docker --format html --output report.html</code>
          </div>
          <p>
            Private HTTPS access uses <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>, <code>GITLAB_TOKEN</code>, or <code>BITBUCKET_TOKEN</code> from the scanner environment. Tokens
            do not enter the clone URL or report. Docker rejects SSH URLs.
          </p>
          <div class="docs-note">
            <strong>Git boundary</strong>
            <p>
              The fetch stage disables hooks, templates, submodule recursion, other protocols, and redirects, and ignores inherited Git configuration. For a
              supplied local
              <code>.git</code> directory, use a separate disposable environment appropriate to your risk and consult Git’s
              <a href="https://git-scm.com/docs/git#_security">untrusted repository guidance ↗</a>
              and
              <a href="https://git-scm.com/docs/git-upload-pack#_security">upload-pack guidance ↗</a>
              for the user boundary behind this advice.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section
          class="docs-section docs-searchable"
          id="edge-cases"
          data-search="edge cases incomplete timeout denied mount malformed oversized json container exit ssh keep workdir multi target"
        >
          <h2>Know the edge cases</h2>
          <div class="docs-table compact" role="table" aria-label="Docker edge cases">
            <div role="row">
              <b role="rowheader">Preflight</b><span role="cell">Missing Docker or pinned image stops before scanning; there is no host fallback.</span>
            </div>
            <div role="row">
              <b role="rowheader">Incomplete target</b
              ><span role="cell"
                >Timeouts, denied mounts, failed fetches, malformed or oversized JSON, and container exits reduce coverage and are reported.</span
              >
            </div>
            <div role="row"><b role="rowheader">SSH</b><span role="cell">SSH URLs are rejected in Docker mode.</span></div>
            <div role="row">
              <b role="rowheader">Keep workdir</b><span role="cell"><code>--keep-workdir</code> is rejected to preserve the checkout and credential boundary.</span>
            </div>
            </div>
          <p>
            For multiple targets, one failed target remains visible as
            <code>SCAN INCOMPLETE</code> while successful targets retain their findings.
          </p>
        </section>
</DocsLayout>
